## Setup
Let $g \equiv 9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, $v_0 := 20\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $k := 0.1\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (drag per unit mass).
Let $\theta := 45°$, $\; \theta \in [5°, 85°]$.

## Without drag
$$ R_0 := \frac{v_0^2 \sin 2\theta}{g} \approx ? \qquad T_0 := \frac{2 v_0 \sin\theta}{g} \approx ? $$

## With linear drag
The equations of motion are $\ddot x = -k \dot x$ and $\ddot y = -g - k \dot y$, with $x(0) = y(0) = 0$, $\dot x(0) = v_0\cos\theta$, $\dot y(0) = v_0 \sin\theta$.

$$ \ddot x = -k\dot x, \quad \ddot y = -g - k\dot y, \quad x(0) = 0,\ y(0) = 0,\ \dot x(0) = v_0\cos\theta,\ \dot y(0) = v_0\sin\theta $$

The ball lands when $y(T) = 0$ with $T > 0$:
$$ T \approx ? \qquad R := x(T) \approx ? $$

Figure: $(x(t), y(t))$ and the drag-free path, $t \in [0, T_0]$. Both paths, with and without drag.

## Discussion
% Drag shortens the range by about 16 percent at this speed. Try moving θ: the best angle with drag is below 45°.
